�  �����  ������  ������  ����  \nEMBROIDERED & \nCROCHETTED GOODS �������  �������  �����  ������  ������  �����  \nSHAWLS AS ARTWARES ����  ����  ����  ����  ����  ����  \nZARI & ZARI GOODS �����  �����  �����  ����  ����  ������  \nIMMITATION JEWELLERY �������  �������  ����  ������  ������  �����  \nMISC. HANDICRAFTS �������  �������  �����  ������  ������  �����  \nTOTAL 10995.42 13005.35 18.28 1712.06 1943.23 13.50 \n  \n \n***** \n  \n  \n�\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_10": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \n����������\n��������������������������\n�����������������������������\n�\n�\n���������������\n�����������������������\nWill the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether a challenge in securing Government funding for water projects is that the \ntendering process favours the lowest-cost bidder as  opposed to providing quality or \nsolution-durability; \n (b) if so, the details thereof and the steps contemplated in this regard; \n (c) whether the absence of economically viable mod els for capital expenditure \nrecovery and operating profitability limits the abi lity of the community water sector \nto attract commercial funding; and \n (d) if so, the details thereof and the steps envis aged by the Union Government to \naddress this issue? �\n�������\n�\n��������������������������������������������������� �\n�����������������������������������\n(a) & (b) Rural Water Supply is a State Subject. Un der the National Rural Drinking \nWater Programme (NRDWP), Ministry of Drinking Water  and Sanitation provides \nfinancial and technical assistance to State Governm ents for rural drinking water \nsupply. States are empowered to plan, design, sanct ion and execute the rural water \nsupply schemes. Although, the Ministry is not invol ved in the tendering process, but \nthe States used to follow the general established p ractice of tendering through which \nthe quality or solution durability of the scheme is ensured by technical clauses before \napplying low cost criteria for awarding rural drink ing water supply contract. \nMoreover, inspections clauses are used to ensure quality issues.  \n(c) & (d) The provision of drinking water supply to  the rural population can neither \nbe characterized as a purely charitable activity no r its model can be based on purely \ncommercial profitability. The States are empowered to choose viable model based on \nthe situations like the density / spread of the pop ulation to be served and spending \ncapability of the population. This has been applied  in few cases including \ncommunity water purification plants and water suppl y schemes conceived with \nexternal aid.   ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_100": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 100 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2017 \n \n \nRevival of Water Bodies \n100. SHRI G.M. SIDDESHWARA: \nWill the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the present status of availability of drinking water in Karnataka throughout the year;  \n(b) whether drinking water is not available to the people of Karnataka since the available \nresources of water such as wells and ponds have dried up; and \n (c) if so, the action plan drawn up by the Governm ent to revive water bodies and \nensuring availability of drinking water through rivers and canals? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n(SHRI RAMESH CHANDAPPA JIGAJINAGI) \n(a) & (b) As per the information furnished by the S tate of Karnataka there are 60,220 \nrural habitations in 176 Taluks out of which 46,118  habitations of 160 Taluks are \ndrought affected. More than 80% of rural water supp ly schemes depend on ground water \nsource.  Successive drought and over exploitation o f ground water by different sectors \nhave affected source and sustainability, due to whi ch coverage is affected and supply \nlevel is drastically reduced and quality is also affected.  \nAs on 28.01.2017, in 465 rural habitations with pop ulation of 4.048 lakhs drinking water \nis supplied through tankers. \n(c) The Karnataka state has taken up 544 numbers of  Multi Village Schemes (MVS) \nwith surface water as source to address coverage an d quality. Sustainability structures \nsuch as Check dams, Percolation tanks, roof top rai n water harvesting structures have \nbeen taken up.  ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_101": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 101 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2017 \n \n \nDrinking Water Crisis \n101. SHRI NISHIKANT DUBEY: \nWill the Minister of DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether drought affected districts / areas of s everal States including Jharkhand \nare facing drinking water scarcity;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UTwise; \n (c) the steps taken by the Union Government for finding solution to drinking water \nscarcity in these districts/areas along with the fu nds allocated on priority basis to \nthese affected areas during each of the last three years and the current year, State/ \nUT-wise; and  \n(d) the details of measures taken by the Union Gove rnment to resolve drinking \nwater scarcity in the country including Jharkhand? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n(SHRI RAMESH CHANDAPPA JIGAJINAGI) \n(a) & (b) The Ministry has not received any intimat ion recently from any State \nincluding Jharkhand that it is facing drinking water scarcity due to drought . \n(c) & (d) This Ministry time to time issues advisor y to the affected States / UTs \nregarding the mitigation measures to be taken up by  the States in the wake of \ndrought / drought like situation. In a latest advis ory to State dated 04.11.2016, the \nstates (including Jharkhand) have been asked to tak e the following measures for \ntackling the crisis of drinking water in the affect ed rural areas: \n a. Hiring of diesel generator sets, pumps. \n b. Hiring of water tankers for emergency water sup ply to affected sites \n subject to recoupment from future releases under c alamity funds. \n\n c. Procurement of sodium hypochlorite, halogen tab lets, bleaching powder \n for proper disinfection. \n d. Hiring of sufficient vehicles fitted with water  treatment plants to provide \n onsite safe drinking water. \n e. Keeping adequate stock of spare parts that may be required for urgent \n repair and restoration works both for piped water supply schemes and  hand \n pumps (tubewells). \n f. Procurement of polyvinyl chloride water tanks f or supply of drinking \n water in relief camps subject to recoupment from f uture releases under \n National Disaster Response Fund / State Disaster R esponse Fund. \n \n              However, under National Rural Drinkin g Water Programme \n(NRDWP), 25 % of the fund is earmarked to States as flexi funds. States can \nuse this fund to take measures to mitigate  drinkin g water scarcity in  \ndrought  situation. Further, States can also utiliz e 15% of Operation and \nMaintenance funds available under NRDWP to meet the  emergent \nrequirement for drinking water mitigation activitie s in the event of drought. \nUnder NRDWP, 2 % of funds are kept aside to be rele ased to States on the \napproval of High Level Committee (HLC)  in the even t of any calamity  \nincluding drought. State / UT wise funds released during last three years and \ncurrent year to meet the drought / drought like sit uation from the calamity \ncomponent of NRDWP is as below: \n(Amount Rs. in crore) \nSr. No. State 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n1 Haryana - 5.05 - - \n2 Karnataka - 9.00 - - \n3 Tamil Nadu 31.00 - - - \n4 Uttar Pradesh  - 44.00 - - \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_102": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES, \nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *102 \nANSWERED ON 09.02.2017 \n \nIRRIGATION PROJECTS \n \n*102.          SHRI K. PARASURAMAN \n \nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the present status of various Extension, Renovation and Modernisation Irrigation projects in the \ncountry including Phase-I of Grand Anicut Canal system project in Tamil Nadu;  \n(b) whether the Technical Advisory Committee has approved these projects and if so, the details \nthereof; and  \n(c) the time by which these projects are likely to be completed? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA \nREJUVENATION  \n(SUSHRI UMA BHARTI) \n(a) to (c)    A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n***** \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART s (a) TO (c) OF STARRED QUESTION \nNO. *102. ANSWERE D ON 09.02.2017 IN LOK SABHA REGARDING “IRRIGATION \nPROJECTS” ASKED BY SHRI K. PARASURAMAN: \n \n (a) to (c)  Central Water Commission (CWC) has reported that out of 32 project proposals under \nappraisal during XII plan, 23 proposals including Grand Anicut Canal system have been accepted \nby the Advisory Committee of this Ministry on Irrigation, Flood Control and Multipurpose projects \nand 4 projects have been sent back due to non- compliance of the observations. The details of 23 \nERM projects accepted by the Advisory Committee is at Annexure -I and the appraisal  status of \nbalance ERM projects is at Annexure – II.   \n  The proposals of Grand Anicut Canal System of Tamil Nadu has been accepted by \nAdvisory  Committee in its 130 th meeting held on 30.09.2016for an estimated cost of Rs. 2298.75 \ncrore with benefits to an area of 1.0 Lakh Ha. However, project authorities have not submitted \nproposal for investment clearance. \n \n Further, Water Resources Projects are planned, funded, executed and maintained by the \nState Governments themselves as per their own resources and priority. In order to supplement the \nefforts of the State Governments, MoWR, RD & GR provides financial assist ance to State \nGovernments to encourage sustainable development and efficient management of water resources \nthrough various schemes such as Accelerated Irrigation Benefits Programme (AIBP) etc. under \nPradhan Manti Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY). Further, inclusion of the projects under various \nschemes for central assistance is considered after all necessary statutory clearances as well as \ninvestment clearance is obtained by project authorities and is subject to fulfillment of conditions \nlaid down in the guidelines of schemes, availability of budget etc.  \n \n Forty two (42) approved ERM projects have been  included under AIBP since its inception. \nCentral Assistance of Rs. 2845.27 crore has been released for these projects upto Dec., 2016. Out \nof these 42 ERM projects, 20 ERM projects have been reported to be completed by State \nGovernments. The issues related to completion of ongoing AIBP projects were considered by a \ncommittee constituted under the Chairmanship of Minister (WR) , Chhattisgarh. The Committee, in \nconsultation with States has identified 99 ongoing AIBP projects including 9 ERM projects for \ncompletion in phases by Dec., 2019 The details of prioritized  9 ERM projects is at Annexure- III.  \n \n \n \n***** \n\n          Annexure-I \nAnnexure-I as mentioned in the part (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 102 regarding Irrigation Projects due for reply on \n09.02.2017 \nLIST OF ERM PROJECTS CONSIDERED AND ACCEPTED BY TECHNICAL ADVISORY COMMITTEE \n              \nSl. Meeting \nNumber \nDate of \nMeeting  \nProject Name Name of the \nState \nEstimated Cost in \ncrore \nBenefits in ha \n1 115th  24.07.2012 Andhra Pradesh Irrigation and Livelihood \nImprovement Project \nAndhra \nPr