    "ls|LS16_S11_U_12": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF YOUTH AFFAIRS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.12 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2017 \n \nNational Service Scheme \n \n†*12.  DR. BANSHILAL MAHATO:  \n \n Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be \npleased to state: \n \n(a)  the salient features and objectives of the National Service \nScheme (NSS);  \n \n(b)  the number of universities and school/  educational \ninstitutions along with the number of students that  are part of \nNSS in the country, State/ UT-wise including Chhattisgarh; and  \n \n(c)  the details of the activities in which students/  participants \nare involved under NSS. \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI VIJAY GOEL) \n \n(a)  to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PAR TS (A) TO (C) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 12 TO BE ANSWERED ON  02.02.2017 \nREGARDING NATIONAL SERVICE SCH EME ASKED BY DR. \nBANSHILAL MAHATO. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n \n(a) National Service Scheme  (NSS) was introduced in \n1969 with the primary objective of developing the \npersonality and character of the student youth \nthrough voluntary community service. ‘Education \nthrough Service’ is the purpose of the NSS. The \nideological orientation of the NSS is inspired by the \nideals of Mahatma Gandhi. Very appropriately, the \nmotto of NSS is “NOT ME, BUT YOU”. An NSS \nvolunteer places the ‘community’ before ‘self’. \n \n NSS is being implemented in Senior Secondary \nSchools, Colleges and Universities. NSS was \nimplemented as a Centrally Sponsored Scheme upto \n2015-16. However, from 01.04.2016, it is being \nimplemented as a Cent ral Sector Scheme. The \nadministration of the Scheme is a collaborative effort \nbetween the Central Government, the States/ UTs and \nthe educational institutions. In each educational \ninstitutions having NSS, there is at least one NSS unit \ncomprising of 100 student volunteers (lesser strength \nin some cases), led by a teacher designated as \nProgramme Officer (PO), who plays a pivotal role as an \neducator, organiser, coordinator, supervisor, \nadministrator and public relations person for the NSS \nunit under him. Each NSS unit adopts a village or slum \nfor taking up its activities. The Programme Officers \nare imparted training through empanelled training \ninstitutions, in order to enable them to discharge their \nduties effectively. In order to administer the \nprogramme effe ctively, there are NSS Cells at \nUniversity level and State level and a Directorate of \nNSS (with regional offices) under the Ministry. In \n\naddition, there are Advisory Committees at various \nlevels, comprising of official and non -official \nmembers, to provide necessary guidance to the NSS \nfunctionaries. \n \nObjectives of NSS : NSS aims at developing the \nfollowing qualities/ competencies among the \nvolunteers: \n \na) To understand the community in which the NSS \nvolunteers work and to understand themselves in \nrelation to their community; \nb) To identify the needs and problems of the \ncommunity and involve themselves in problem -\nsolving exercise; \nc) To develop among themselves a sense of social \nand civic responsibility; \nd) To utilize their knowledge in finding practical \nsolutions to individual and community problems; \ne) To gain skills in mobilizing community \nparticipation; \nf) To acquire leadership qualities and democratic \nvalues; \ng) To develop capacity to meet emergencies and \nnatural disasters; and  \nh) To practice national integration and social \nharmony. \n \nNSS attempts to establish meaningful linkages \nbetween ‘Campus and Community’, ‘College and \nVillage’ and ‘Knowledge and Action’. \n \n(b) NSS has about 36.58 lakh volunteers enrolled in 39,695 \nNSS units spread over 391 Universities/ +2 Councils, \n16,278 Colleges/ Technical Institutions and 12,483 \nSenior Secondary Schools . The State/ UT -wise breakup \nof enrolment , including that of Chhattisgarh , is given at \nAnnexure-I. \n\n \n(c) Each NSS volunteer is required to put in minimum 120 \nhours of service per year for two years, i.e., total 240 \nhours. This work is undertaken in villages/ slums \nadopted by NSS unit or in school/ college campuses, \nusually after study hours or during weekends/ \nvacations.  In addit ion, each volunteer is required to \nattend at least one Special Camp of 7 days’ duration \nduring the 2-year period, organised by the NSS unit, in the \nadopted villages/ urban slums.  The activities under NSS \ncontinue to evolve in response to needs of the comm unity. \nSome of the areas of activity are  literacy and education, \nhealth &  family welfare , sanitation and cleanliness , \nenvironment conservation, women’s empowerment, \ndisaster relief and rehabilitation, etc. NSS volunteers play \nan important role in creating awareness about development \nprogrammes and against social evils. In addition to these \ncore activities, NSS volunteer s also participate in some \nother important activities like Republic Day Parade, \nAdventure Camps, National Integration Camps and North \nEast NSS Festivals, ‘Suvichar’ and ‘Youth Convention’ \nevents during the National Youth Festival, Self -Defence \nTraining for NSS Volunteers, etc. \n \n***** \n\nAnnexure-I\nStatement indicating Number of Educational Institutions covered under NSS alongwith No. of NSS \nVolunteers, State/ UT-wise (as on 31.03.2016)\nUniversites Colleges/ \nTech. \nInstitutions\nSchools Total\n1 A & N Islands 0 5 12 17 1482\n2 Andhra Pradesh 23 1663 0 1686 206000\n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1 26 41 68 13000\n4 Assam 5 214 0 219 37088\n5 Bihar 15 401 150 566 66963\n6 Chandigarh 3 22 59 84 16200\n7 Chhattisgarh 6 369 737 1112 104376\n8 D & N Haveli 0 0 10 10 1100\n9 Daman a