    "ls|LS16_S11_U_46": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF CULTURE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n†\n*46\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2017\n \n \n \nPROMOTION OF ART AND CULTURE\n \n \n†\n*46\n. \nSHRIMATI JYOTI DHURVE\n: \n \n \nWill the Minister of CULTURE be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \nthe major initiatives taken to \npromote art and culture in the country;\n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government has decided to accord priority in the fields of arts, music, \ndance and literature and if so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(c)\n \nwhether the Government has received any proposal for providing assis\ntance to the \nactive groups engaged in the field of music and also promote the significance of \nculture and language amongst the youth in the recent years and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n \n(d)\n \nthe follow\n-\nup action taken thereon?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STAT\nE (IC) FOR CULTURE & TOURISM \n \n(DR. MAHESH SHARMA)\n \n \n \n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n†\n*46\n \nDUE FOR ANSWER ON 06.02.2017.\n \n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nPromot\nion of Art and Culture is a continuous process and is undertaken \nthrough various schemes, festivals, training programmes, seminars, \nworkshops, drama performances, exhibitions etc. organized across the \ncountry. In the mid 1980s, Zonal Cultural Centres (ZCCs\n) were  set\n-\nup by the \ngovernment with a view to develop the various folk art and culture of various \nregions and also to set up mechanism for preservation and promotion of \nvarious elements of our rich cultural heritage. The ZCCs were required to \nconcentrate\n \non the folk arts, dance and music. The mandate behind setting \nup the ZCCs was to bind the nation culturally while retaining the individuality \nof the regions that comprise them. \n \n \n \n \nIn addition, various autonomous organizations under the administrative \ncon\ntrol of the M/o Culture have performed inter alia, the following functions:\n-\n \n \n(\ni\n)\n \nSangeet Natak Akademi (SNA) has been functioning as the apex body \nin the field of performing arts in the country, preserving and promoting the \nvast intangible heritage of the Ind\nia’s diverse culture express in forms of \nmusic, dance and drama.  \n \n \n(\nii\n)\n \nApart from its teaching activities, National School of Drama (NSD) \nthrough its repertory and Theatre\n-\nin\n-\nEducation Companies takes various \ninitiatives to promote Indian culture by performin\ng plays for public in various \nparts of the country. \n \n \n(\niii\n)\n \nSahitya Akademi (SA) is engaged in preserving, promoting and \nenriching the literature of India. To this end the Akademi publishes large \nvolumes of material, confers a number of awards and grants, conduc\nts \nnumerous programmes, seminars, workshops, lectures, symposia, readings, \ndiscussions and performances in all the 24 languages recognized by it.\n \n \n(\niv\n)\n \nCentre for Cultural Resources and Training (CCRT) undertakes various \nactivities for promotion and disseminati\non educationally of Arts and Culture \nin the country. \n \n \n(\nv\n)\n \nIndira Gandhi National Centre for the Arts (IGNCA) is working to \npresent Indian Arts and Culture in its totality. Two major Projects titled \n“Vedic Heritage Portal” and “National Culture for Audio\n-\nvisua\nl Archives”, \nunder the aegis of the Ministry of Culture have been initiated to preserve and \npresent the Indian tradition, available in Audiovisual and Literary forms.\n \n \n(\nvi\n)\n \nLalit Kala Akademi (LKA) has been promoting creative endeavours of \nIndian artists and br\ninging their arts closer to a large number of audience; \nthereby playing an important role in defining and redefining the sensibility of \nIndia’s visual culture. \n \n \n\n(\nvii\n)\n \nKalakshetra Foundation (KF) has been carrying out various activities \nfor promotion and dissemi\nnation of knowledge on Bharatanatyam, Carnatic \nMusic, Visual Arts and crafts, etc.\n \n \n \n(c)&(d):\n \n \nMinistry of Culture administers various cultural schemes under which \nfinancial assistance is provided to the individual/ groups, State Governments, \nState sponsor\ned bodies, Universities, local bodies and other Government \napproved agencies including reputed (not\n-\nfor profit) cultural organizations, \nbased on the proposals/applications received from them.\n \n \n \nA number of groups being represented by youth of India are bei\nng supported \nby the Sangeet Natak Akademi by way of sanction of grants and giving them \nopportunities to perform in the different programmes of the Akademi under \nAkademi’s Grant\n-\nin\n-\naid schemes and other schemes.\n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_47": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 47\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2017\nPENDING PROPOSALS UNDER PMGSY\n47. SHRI GODSE HEMANT TUKARAM:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to upgrade the roads constructed under the Pradhan\nMantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) after their guarantee period is over;\n(b) if so, the details thereof;\n(c) the total amount of funds released by the Government for upgradation of such roads\nduring the last three years, State/UTwise including Maharashtra and Gujarat;\n(d) the details of proposals received from State Governments for upgradation of rural\nroads under PMGSY which are pending with t he Union Government as on date,\nState/UT-wise; and\n(e) the time by which these pending proposals are likely to be cleared?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a) to (e): Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana-II (PMGSY-II) launched in May, 2013,\nenvisages consolidation of the existing Rural Road Network to improve its overall efficiency\nas a provider of transportation services for people, goods and services.  It aims to cover\nupgradation of existing sele cted rural roads based on their economic potential and their role\nin facilitating the growth of rural market centres and rural hubs. Roads already\nconstructed/upgraded under PMGSY-I also become eligible for PMGSY -II on completion of\ntheir design life i.e. Ten years and on the basis of requirement to accommodate enhanced\ntraffic, if it has gone beyond the projected traffic and such traffic cannot be accommodated in\nthe existing carriageway width, with the required level of service. The deteriorated condition\nof the road due to non-maintenance or non-undertaking of renewal works at the required time\ncannot be the sole  criteria for taking the roads of PMGSY-I in PMGSY-II.\nA target length of 50,000 Km has been fixed among the States, including Maharashtra and\nGujarat under PMGSY-II. The Ministry has sanctioned 2618.90 Km and 1180.31 Km of road\nlength to Maharashtra and Gujarat State, respectively. State -wise details of proposals\nreceived and cleared during 2016 -17under PMGSY are at Annexure-I. State-wise details of\nfunds released under PMGSY (including Maharashtra and Gujarat) during the last three years\nare at Annexure-II.\n*****\n\nAnnexure - II\nReleases  under PMGSY (Rs in crore)\n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16\nSl. No. State Release Release Release\n1 Andhra Pradesh 5.00 32.98 379.20\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 8.00 345.92 375.00\n3 Assam 240.49 316.07 347.82\n4 Bihar 850.83 1548.16 2781.00\n5 Chattisgarh 0.00 270.75 498.00\n6 Goa 0.00 0.00 0.00\n7 Gujarat 519.24 418.77 474.10\n8 Haryana 0.00 218.96 304.69\n9 Himachal Pradesh 0.00 99.40 268.40\n10 Jammu & Kashmir 523.24 416.60 488.00\n11 Jharkhand 21.86 249.48 864.99\n12 Karnataka 5.00 237.00 140.80\n13 Kerala 1.50 151.41 151.00\n14 Madhya Pradesh 615.00 708.00 1122.00\n15 Maharashtra 0.00 212.53 553.30\n16 Manipur 4.03 100.00 299.80\n17 Meghalaya 0.00 62.56 150.70\n18 Mizoram 0.00 54.74 50.90\n19 Nagaland 0.00 58.99 4.00\n20 Orissa 758.92 1051.50 1382.70\n21 Punjab 117.68 310.21 221.10\n22 Rajasthan 427.06 425.66 559.90\n23 Sikkim 1.97 94.59 68.60\n24 Tamil Nadu 343.48 239.65 205.00\n25 Tripura 98.83 187.36 274.83\n26 Uttar Pradesh 511.93 638.70 1110.35\n27 Uttarakhand 0.00 314.92 409.19\n28 West Bengal 306.17 1193.80 1427.58\n29 Telangana 0.00 0.88 273.73\nTotal 5360.24 9959.58 15186.68\n\nAnnexure - I\nAnnexure referred in reply of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 47 due for 02.02.2017\nDetails of Proposals received and cleared under PMGSY  in 2016-17\nSl\nNo State\nProposed Cleared\nRemarksNo. of\nworks\nValue Rs.\nin Crores\nNo. of\nworks\nValue Rs.\nin Crores\n1 Arunachal Pradesh 215 2493.84 155 1488.56\n2 Assam 287 580.68 105 197.73 Clearance issued for Batch-I of 2016-17. Empowered Committee\nmeeting for  Batch-II of 2016-17  held on 13.01.2017.\n3 Bihar 2197 3032.51 1401 1716.90 Clearance issued for Batch-I of 2016-17. Pre-Empowered Committee\nmeeting for  Batch-II of 2016-17 held on 17.01.2017.\n4 Chhatisgarh 318 841.50 610 1454.78 Including proposals received during 2015-16\n5 Gujarat - - 44 50.81 Proposals received during 2015-16\n6 Haryana 5 39.16 5 39.16\n7 Himachal Pradesh 350 1200.33 190 649.48 Empwered Committee meeting to consider proposals under Batch-II of\n2016-17 held on 13.1.2017.\n8 Jammu and\nKashmir 564 3260.69 542 2842.32\n9 Jharkhand 1541 2518.69 848 1361.95 Clearance issued for Batch-I & II of 2016-17. Empowered Committee\nmeeting for Batch-III of 2016-17 held on 28.12.2016.\n10 Kerala - - 84 274.2 Received and proposed during 2015-16.\n11 Madhaya Pradesh 722 1318.20 1048 1842.81 Including proposals received during 2015-16\n12 Mizoram 56 726.97 56 553.54\n13 Odisha 2233 4664.75 1368 2848.31 Clearance issued for Batch-I & II of 2016-17. Empowered Committee\nmeeting for Batch-III of 2016-17 held on 13.01.2017\n14 Rajasthan - - 1467 1611.92 Received and proposed during 2015-16.\n15 Sikkim 125 490.69 125 490.69\n16 Tamil Nadu - - 595 758.44 Received and proposed during 2015-16.\n17 Telangana 154 241.43 - - Empowered Committee meeting held on 01.12.2016.\n18 Tripura 39 115.21 39 111.39\n19 Uttar Pradesh 714 3477.54 568 2596.37\n20 Uttarakhand 105 579.44 189 989.96 Including proposals received during 2015-16\n21 West Bengal 1094 3394.08 143 697.97 Pre Empowered Committee Meeting held on 17.01.2017.\n22 Manipur 244 1278.17\n23 Punjab 3 9.23 Pre Empowered Committee Meeting  held on 16.01.2017\nTotal 10966 30263.11 9582 22577.29",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_48": "O.I.H.\nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 48\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2017\nRURAL ROAD CONNECTIVITY\n48. SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the amount of funds sanctioned and released by the Union Government to Bihar under\nthe Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) during the last three years;\n(b) the number of proposals received from Bihar which are pending wit h Union\nGovernment under the said scheme and the time by which such proposals are likely to\nbe cleared;\n(c) the time by which all rural areas of the country are linked with all weather roads and\nwhether any deadline has been fixed for the same; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI RAM KRIPAL YADAV)\n(a): ‘Rural Roads’ is a State subject and Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY)\nis a one-time special intervention to provide rural connectivity, by way of a single all-weather\nroad, to the eligible unconnected habitations in the core network. Under PMGSY funds\nreleased to Bihar during the last three years are as under:\n(Rs. in Crore)\n(b): As per PMGSY guidelines, the State Governments are required to submit Detailed\nProject Reports (DPRs) to the Ministry for consideration/approval of projects under\nPMGSY. The project proposals are scrutinized by National Rural Roads Development\nAgency (NRR DA) to ensure that the proposals are in accordance with the programme\nguidelines and that they have been duly verified by the State Technical Agencies (STAs). The\nproposals of the State are then put up before the Empowered Committee chaired by Secretary\n(RD) in the Ministry of Rural Development, for its consideration. During the current financial\nyear, Ministry has sanctioned 1331 roads and 70 bridges of length 2759.65 km and 5442.52\nmtr. respectively to Bihar . The State has also submitted a proposal of 6 60 roads covering a\nlength of 1379.56 km and 136 bridges in the Pre Empowered Committee meeting held on 17 th\nJanuary, 2017.\nYear Funds released\n2013-14 850.83\n2014-15 1548.16\n2015-16 2781.00\n\n(c) & (d): Out of 1,83,599 eligible unconnected habitations across the States under\nPMGSY, 1,56,273 habitations have been sanctione d by the Ministry, out of which States\nhave reported to have connected 1,21,241 habitations upto December, 2016. As per the recent\nadvisory of the Ministry of Rural Development, the State Governments have been requested\nto get approval for sanctions of bal ance works under PMGSY -I/II as per mandate of the\nscheme and complete them latest by March, 2019 so as to provide connectivity to target\nhabitations.\n*****",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_49": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 49 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2017 \n \nIMPACT OF DEMONETISATION ON JOB MARKET \n \n*49. SHRI MUTHAMSETTI SRINIVASA RAO (AVANTHI): \n        DR. BHOLA SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)the total number of daily wage labourers employed in the \nGovernment as well as private sector in the country, State/UT-wise; \n(b)whether the unorganised sector in the country has witnessed a \nhuge job loss due to demonetisation; \n(c)if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken/being taken by \nthe Government to protect the unorganized sector from further job \nloss; \n(d)whether the Government has conducted any study to assess the \nimpact of demonetisation on the job market and if so, the details \nand the outcome thereof along with the number of workers/jobs \naffected or likely to be affected as a result thereof; and \n(e)the remedial steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government \nin this regard? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n*                                                  ******* \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS  (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 49 FOR ANSWER ON 06.02.2017 \nREGARDING IMPACT OF DEMONETISATION ON JOB MARKET.  \n \n(a) Daily wage labourers are engaged directly by the establishments \nbased on their respective needs. Data regarding total number o f daily \nwage labourers employed in the Government as well as private sector  is \nnot centrally maintained.  \n \n(b) to (d) There is no such input available with this Ministry nor any study \nhas been conducted in this regard.  \n \n(e) Question does not arise in view of the reply above.  \n \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_5": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 5\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.02.2017\nGUIDELINES UNDER PMGSY\n*5. SHRI HARI OM PANDAY:\nDR. RATNA DE (NAG):\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the status of construction of roads under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY),\nState-wise;\n(b) the norms/guidelines in vogue to measure roads constructed under the scheme;\n(c) whether the Government has proposed new Guidelines for road measurement under the\nscheme;\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor along with the steps proposed to be taken in\nthis regard; and\n(e) whether the Government has assessed the likely impact the new guidelines shall have on\nconstruction of rural roads in the country and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n\nThe Statement as referred to in the Answer to Lok Sabha Starred Question No 5\nState-wise details of roads constructed under Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana\n(PMGSY) are given at Annexure-1.\nThe roads under PMGSY are constructed and measured as per the Specifications for\nRural Roads of Ministry of Rural Development published by Indian Roads Congress (IRC)\nand other relevant IRC guidelines. T hough t he book of specifications has been revised by\nIRC in 2014, no changes have been made for measurements of items of road.\nThe Government has not made new Guidelines for road measurement under PMGSY.\nHence the question of assessing the likely impact of such guidelines does not arise.\n\nAnnxure-1\nAnnexure referred in reply to  the Lok Sabha Starred Question.No. 5  due for\n02.02.2017\nSl.No. State Name Length of road\nworks\nsanctioned (in\nkm)\nLength of road\nworks completed\n(upto December,\n2016) (in km)\n1 Andhra Pradesh 14,747.09 13,574.62\n2 Arunachal Pradesh 8,200.88 5,491.33\n3 Assam 17,923.65 16,275.98\n4 Bihar 52,719.92 40,877.79\n5 Chhattisgarh 32,132.54 26,719.98\n6 Goa 178.42 155.33\n7 Gujarat 12,720.62 12,343.70\n8 Haryana 5,614.34 5,528.42\n9 Himachal Pradesh 14,632.62 11,938.29\n10 Jammu And Kashmir 13,669.21 6,813.58\n11 Jharkhand 21,866.43 13,972.65\n12 Karnataka 18,605.61 18,378.54\n13 Kerala 3,534.89 2,733.77\n14 Madhya Pradesh 74,915.32 64,126.03\n15 Maharashtra 27,039.73 24,314.28\n16 Manipur 6,720.13 5,453.13\n17 Meghalaya 2,216.31 1,436.03\n18 Mizoram 3,507.85 2,499.03\n19 Nagaland 3,629.63 3,457.37\n20 Odisha 51,053.11 38,608.07\n21 Punjab 8,284.27 7,035.21\n22 Rajasthan 66,382.03 59,192.07\n23 Sikkim 4,189.20 3,159.26\n24 Tamilnadu 15,295.86 13,492.94\n25 Tripura 4,867.37 3,777.62\n26 Uttar Pradesh 57,396.81 49,956.81\n27 Uttarakhand 10,263.48 7,398.48\n28 West Bengal 26,560.53 21,749.46\n29 Telangana 10,640.66 9,727.88\nTotal States 589,508.52 490,187.65",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_50": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 50 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 06.02.2017 \n \nTRIPARTITE COMMITTEE ON CONVENTION \n \n*50. SHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \n        SHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the 38th meeting of the Tripartite Committee on \nConvention was held in New Delhi recently to take stock of the \nprogress made in respect of various ILO conventions and Report on \nthe working group on standard review mechanism; \n(b)if so, the details thereof along with the issues discussed therein; \n(c)whether progress in regard to certain conventions was not found \nsatisfactory; \n(d)if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n(e)the steps taken by the Government for ratification of international \nlabour standards and recommending the future road map? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n*                                                  ******* \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO  PARTS (a) TO (e) OF  LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 50 DUE FOR 06/02/2017 BY SHRI \nSHRIRANG APPA BARNE AND SHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT \nREGARDING TRIPARTITE COMMITTEE ON CONVENTION. \n \n \n(a) Yes, Madam. \n \n(b) The 38 th meeting of the Tripartite  Committee on Convention \n(COC) was held, on 10.1.2017, at New Delhi, under the \nchairpersonship of Secretary, Ministry of Labour and \nEmployment.  Representatives of Central Trade Unions, \nEmployers’ Organizations, State Governments, Central \nMinistries, International Labour Organization and officers from \nthe Ministry of Labour & Employment participated in the \nmeeting. The agenda of the COC meeting consisted of \ndiscussions on: \n(i) ILO Convention No. 138 and 182 on Child Labour, \n(ii) ILO Convention No. 87 regarding Freedom of Association \nand protection of Rights and ILO Convention No. 98 \nregarding Right to Organize and Collective Bargaining, \n(iii) Maritime Labour Convention 2006, Convention No. 185 \nregarding Seafarers’ Identify Document, \n(iv) ILO Convention No. 187 regarding promotional Framework \nfor Occupational Safety and Health, \n(v) ILO Convention No. 153 regarding Hours of work and Rest \nPeriods in Road Transport \n(vi) Report on the Working Group on Standard Review \nMechanism.   \n(c) The COC discussed the status and progress on ratification of \nILO Conventions.  \n(d) Not Applicable. \n(e) Ratification of an ILO Convention is a voluntary process. India \nratifies a Convention only when our national laws and \npractices are in full conformity with the Convention.  \n \n***** \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_51": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n                MINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n \nLOK SABHA  \n                UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.51 \n             ANSWERED ON 2nd FEBRUARY, 2017 \nWATER TRANSPORT PROJECTS  \n†51.  SHRI RAJAN VICHARE:  \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING  be pleased to state: \nपोतप�रवहनमंत् \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to develop water transport \nsystem in various towns like Navi Mumbai, Thane etc. located \nalong the coastal areas in the country;  \n(b)  if so, the details and the present status thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor;  \n(c)the details and present status of Thane water transport project;  \n(d)  the time frame proposed for execution of the said project; and  \n(e)  the steps taken/being taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a)to(c):To develop the Inland Water Transport ( IWT) in Navi Mumbai, \nThane areas of Mumbai region, the Kalyan–  Thane– Mumbai \nWaterways, Vasai Creek and river Ulhas, have been declared as \nNW-53 under the National Waterways Act, 2016.   The \nMaharashtra Maritime Board has undertaken preparation of a \nDetailed Project Report ( DPR)through the Thane Municipal \nCorporation for the development of Thane creek which will \nconnect Ulhasnagar, Kalyan –Dombivali, Bhiwandi, Thane, \nMira–Bhayander, Vasai –Virar Municipal Corporation areas in \nMumbai Metropolitan Region (MMR).  \n \n(d)to(e):The timeframe for execution of the above project would be clear \nafter finalization of the DPR. \n*****\n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_52": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT  \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  52 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 6TH FEBRUARY, 2017 \n \nFunds for Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan \n \n*52. SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI: \n        SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA:  \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of funds allocat ed and spent under the Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) in \nvarious States during the last three years along with the percen tage spent on quality upgradation \nmeasures, State/UT-wise; \n(b) whether criteria have been fixed by the Government for providing higher proportion of funds \nto the States/UTs under SSA and if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the number of toilets constructed in school s both Government and Public during the year \n2015-16 and the number of functional toilets out of these;  \n(d) whether as per some recent reports, the implementation of SSA a nd Mid Day Meal schemes \nis far from satisfactory and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(e) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to improve am enities in schools viz, the \nquality of mid day meal, separate  toilets for girls in schools, learning outcomes and teachers' \nabsenteeism, multiple vacancies etc. across the country, especially in Uttar Pradesh? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n \n(a) to (e):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 52 TO BE ANSWERED ON 6 TH FEBRUARY, 2017 ASKED \nBY SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI AND SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA REGARDING \nFUNDS FOR SARVA SHIKSHA ABHIYAN \n \n(a): The Statement showing state-wise details of funds released and expenditure under Sarva \nShiksha Abhiyan (SSA) during the last three years and current year is given at Annexure. Under \nthe SSA, the central share is released in lump-sum directly  to States and UTs and component \nwise releases are not made. Fo r the financial year 2016-17, 10%  of the total outlay has been \napproved for activities related to quality interv entions through SSA in the Annual Plans for all \nStates and UTs. All States and UTs have been advised to priori ties their expenditure on such \ninterventions and have been given specific co mmitments focused on improvement in learning \noutcomes during the finalization of their Annual Plans under SSA for 2016-17.   \n \n(b): The outlays to States and UTs under SSA ar e made on the basis of  their Annual Work \nPlan and Budget (AWP&B) as per the programmatic and financial norms.  The approved outlay \nis apportioned between central and state share as per th e existing funds sharing pattern of  60:40 \n(90:10 for the North-Eastern Stat es and 3 Himalayan States). 100%  central share is provided to \nUnion Territories.  \n(c): Under the Swachh Vidyalaya initiative of  the Department, 4,17,796 toilets were \nconstructed in 2,61,400 elementary and secondary government schools in a period of one year \nfrom 15.8.2014 to 15.8.2015, thus ensuring that every government school has separate functional \ntoilets for girls and boys. Out of these, 1,41,636 toile ts were constructed/repaired by PSUs. With \nthis, 13.77 crore children in 11.21 lakh government  schools all over the country were given \naccess to gender segregated toilets. \n(d):  No, Madam.  \n (e):  The SSA provides assistance to Stat e Governments and UT Administrations for \nuniversalization of elementary education in the country. Since the inception of SSA in 2000-01 \ntill 30.09.2016, 3.64 lakh new elementary schools, construction of 3.11 lakh school buildings and \n18.73 lakh additional classrooms, 2.42 lakh drinking water faciliti es, 10.36 lakh sc hool toilets \nand 19.49 lakh teacher posts have been sanctioned to States and UTs.  Out of these, 3.59 lakh \nelementary schools have been opened, 3.02 lakh school buildings, 18.37 lakh additional \nclassrooms, 2.33 lakh drinking water facilities and 9.88 lakh toilets have been constructed. \n  \n \n  \n\n \n \nFurther, under SSA, the State Government s and UT administrations are supported on \nseveral interventions to improve teaching sta ndards, including regular in-service teachers’ \ntraining, induction training for newly recruited t eachers, training of all untrained teachers to \nacquire professional qualifications, recruitm ent of additional teachers for improving pupil-\nteacher ratios, academic support for teachers through block and cluster resource centers, \ncontinuous and comprehensive evaluation syst em to equip the teacher to measure pupil \nperformance and provide remedial action wherever  required, and teacher and school grants for \ndevelopment of appropriate teaching-learning materi al, etc. States have been advised to monitor \nteacher attendance in elementary schools and put  in place mechanisms to improve teacher \npresence and accountability.  \nThe recruitment and service conditions of Government teachers are primarily in the \ndomain of respective State Governments and UT Administrations. Central Government regularly \ntakes up the issue of filling up of vacant teacher posts and redeployment of teachers with the \nState Government and UTs. \nAdditionally, to improve the qua lity of elementary education in the country, the Central \nGovernment has taken several initiatives. Padhe  Bharat Badhe Bharat (PBB) which is a sub-\nprogramme under Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA), encourage children to improve on early grade \nreading, writing & comprehension, and early Mathematics in classes I and II. Rashtriya \nAavishkar Abhiyan (RAA) progra mme has been launched in July 2015, inter-alia, as a sub-\ncomponent of Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA), to motivate and engage children of the age group \nfrom 6-18 years in Science, Mathematics a nd Technology through observation, experimentation, \ninference drawing, model buildi ng, etc. both through inside and outside classroom activities.\n  To \nfurther strengthen the emphasis on quality education, the lear ning outcomes for students at \nelementary level have been finalized and they will  be included in the Central Rules of the Right \nof Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE), Act, 2009. \nIn respect of Mid Day Meal Scheme (MDM S), the Government has issued various \ninstructions from time to time to maintain the quality of m eals served to the children under \nMDMS. Updated guidelines on qual ity, safety and hygiene for school  level kitchens have been \nissued to all the States and UTs on 13 th February, 2015. These guideline s, inter alia, provide for \nprocurement of Agmark quality a nd branded items for preparation of  meals, tasting of meals by \n2-3 adults members including at least one teacher before serving to children. Further, the MDM \nRules, 2015 provide for mandatory testing of food samples on monthly basis by Government \nrecognized laboratories to ensure that the meals meet with nutritional standards and quality. \n  \n\n \n \nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO.52 TO BE ANSWERED ON 6TH FEBRUARY, 2017 ASKED BY SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI AND \nSHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA REGARDING FUNDS FOR SARVA SHIKSHA ABHIYAN \n \nState-wise funds allocated/released and expenditure under Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) during last three years \nand the current year \n(Rs. in crore) \nSl. \nNo. Name of the State \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nFunds \nReleased \nExpenditure  \n(including \nState share) \nFunds \nReleased \nExpenditure  \n(including \nState share) \nFunds \nReleased \nExpenditure  \n(including \nState share) \nFunds \nReleased \n(as on \n04.01.2017) \nExpenditure \n(including \nState share) \n(till Sept., \n2016) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 1747.15 2813.02 1545.67 2201.52 668.11 1610.52 633.02 355.40 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 192.62 279.51 336.08 354.17 181